---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/manganese`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Manganese Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Manganese', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Manganese: SOD Cofactor, Bone Formation, and Manganism Risk"
  description="Manganese reference — Adequate Intake, MnSOD antioxidant defence, well-water and welding-fume overexposure (manganism), and food sources."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/manganese", label: "Manganese" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Manganese</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Manganese is a cofactor for manganese superoxide dismutase (MnSOD, a mitochondrial
      antioxidant), pyruvate carboxylase, and glutamine synthetase. Deficiency in
      humans is essentially unknown; toxicity, on the other hand, is a real occupational
      and environmental problem — chronic overexposure produces a Parkinson-like disorder
      called manganism.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily Adequate Intake</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adult men:</strong> 2.3 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Adult women:</strong> 1.8 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 2.0 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 2.6 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 11 mg/day for adults from food and supplements combined.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Toxicity — manganism</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Chronic inhalational or ingestion overexposure causes manganese accumulation in the
      basal ganglia, producing a Parkinson-like syndrome — bradykinesia, rigidity, gait
      disturbance, dystonic postures, and psychiatric changes. Historically an
      occupational disease of miners and steel welders; today the main risk groups are:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Welders</strong> with long-term inhalation of welding fumes.</li>
      <li><strong>Chronic liver disease</strong> — manganese is excreted in bile; hepatic failure causes accumulation.</li>
      <li><strong>Total parenteral nutrition</strong> — IV manganese bypasses homeostasis; TPN patients need careful monitoring.</li>
      <li><strong>Well-water consumers</strong> in some regions where groundwater manganese exceeds 300 µg/L; associated with impaired cognition in children.</li>
      <li><strong>Ephedrone/methcathinone users</strong> (drug production uses potassium permanganate; contamination is severe).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The bone-formation claim</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Manganese is a cofactor for glycosyltransferases involved in bone matrix formation.
      Some "bone health" supplements combine manganese with calcium and vitamin D. There
      is no evidence that manganese supplementation above dietary intake improves bone
      mineral density in humans with normal manganese status.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Mussels (3 oz): 5.8 mg — extreme outlier</li>
      <li>Hazelnuts (1 oz): 1.6 mg</li>
      <li>Pecans (1 oz): 1.3 mg</li>
      <li>Brown rice (1 cup cooked): 1.1 mg</li>
      <li>Oatmeal (1 cup cooked): 1.4 mg</li>
      <li>Whole wheat bread (1 slice): 0.5 mg</li>
      <li>Pineapple (½ cup): 0.8 mg</li>
      <li>Tea (1 cup): 0.4–0.5 mg — chronic heavy tea intake can contribute significantly</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Manganese is one supplement to leave alone —
      dietary intake covers the AI easily, and the risk-benefit ratio of supplementation
      is unfavourable outside of well-defined TPN or malabsorption scenarios.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/manganese" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
